::PC_U03_L02_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L02_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.8
Question:
On the unit circle, which coordinate gives cosine?
{
=A. x-coordinate#Correct. A unit circle point is written as (cosine, sine).
~B. y-coordinate#The y-coordinate gives sine, not cosine.
~C. radius squared#The radius is 1, but radius squared is not cosine.
~D. angle label only#The angle label helps locate the point, but the x-coordinate gives cosine.
}
::PC_U03_L02_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L02_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.8
Question:
For the unit circle point (1/2, sqrt(3)/2), what is sine?
{
~A. 1/2#This is cosine because it is the x-coordinate.
=B. sqrt(3)/2#Correct. Sine is the y-coordinate, sqrt(3)/2.
~C. 2#This is not a coordinate value from the point.
~D. sqrt(3)#This ignores the denominator in the y-coordinate.
}
::PC_U03_L02_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U03_L02_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.8
Question:
If sin(theta)=3/5 and cos(theta)=4/5, what is tan(theta)?
{
~A. 4/3#This is cosine/sine, which is cotangent.
~B. 5/3#This does not use the tangent ratio sine/cosine.
=C. 3/4#Correct. tangent = sine/cosine = (3/5)/(4/5) = 3/4.
~D. 7/5#This adds values instead of forming the tangent ratio.
}
::PC_U03_L02_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U03_L02_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.8
Question:
Which function is the reciprocal of cosine?
{
~A. sine#Sine is a coordinate, not the reciprocal of cosine.
~B. tangent#Tangent is sine/cosine.
~C. cosecant#Cosecant is the reciprocal of sine.
=D. secant#Correct. Secant is 1/cosine when cosine is not 0.
}
::PC_U03_L02_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U03_L02_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.8
Question:
When is tangent undefined on the unit circle?
{
=A. When cosine is 0#Correct. Tangent is sine/cosine, so it is undefined when cosine is 0.
~B. When sine is 0#If sine is 0, tangent equals 0 when cosine is not 0.
~C. When radius is 1#The radius is always 1 on the unit circle and does not make tangent undefined.
~D. When the angle is positive#Positive angles can have defined or undefined tangent depending on cosine.
}